    feasiblity: prospects for refuting the claim of a necessarily tight coupling
      between habitability and safeholding capacity
        : re `claim of.+coupling between (habitability) and (safeholding) capacity`p see
          `^*• the conditions of ${1} and.+${2} are bound together` @ `^^multiverse ensemble$`i
          @ ../text.brec
        : cf. `^*- prospects for refuting.+: hardly could life exist absent a speed limit on causation$`
          @ 01.logic_tour/._/60.vetting_their_explanantia.notes.brec
        ⇒ better to neutralize the threat and win the issue over to my favour by giving an alternative
          (or being agnostic, additional) explanans, one that is more convincing than necessity
            - viz. design, including my version of it
            - explains well the tight coupling between habitability and safeholding capacity
                / what is the key premise of (rival explanans) the multiverse ensemble
                - explains it better, I think, undercutting that rival
                - explains it as a product of the ramifications involved in adding habitability
                  atop a foundation of safeholding
                    - viz. in building it atop, fitting|moulding it onto, that foundation
                    : cf. https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1995QJRAS..36..193H : ‘An intricate network
                      of interlocking critical relations leaves the investigator puzzled by what
                      appears to be evidence that the universe has been designed at a fundamental
                      (not superficial) physical level for the benefit of organic life.’  Harrison,
                      Edward R. 1995. ‘The natural selection of universes containing intelligent life.’
                      *Quarterly journal of the Royal Astronomical Society* 36, no. 3.  p. 195.
